{"availability":"available","full":"celox-sim/celox","name":"celox","description":"Celox HDL Simulator","category":"Other","language":"Rust","stars":10,"forks":1,"created":"2026-02-28T11:52:19Z","updated":"2026-10-04T08:11:33Z","license":"Apache-2.0","branch":"master","homepage":"https://celox-sim.github.io/celox/","topics":[],"demo":"https://celox-sim.github.io/celox/playground/","color":"#323057","ink":"#d6caff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-04T08:15:58.621Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-04T08:15:58.621Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-04T08:15:58.621Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-03T22:13:41.081Z","stars":10,"forks":1},{"at":"2026-10-04T08:15:58.621Z","stars":10,"forks":1}],"technologies":["Rust"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Celox compiles an elaborated Veryl design into executable simulation kernels and exposes the design through a type-safe TypeScript API. It is both a practical way to test RTL with Vitest and an open testbed for exploring how RTL simulators should be structured.","Celox explores a simple question: what does a modern RTL simulator architecture look like when compilation, scheduling, state representation, code generation, and testbench integration are designed together?","This makes Celox useful as an architecture laboratory without reducing it to a compiler demo: the result can run real RTL tests, in Node.js or in a browser."],"features":["Veryl-specific analysis ends at a source-independent design representation.","Combinational dependencies and clock domains are scheduled before execution.","A backend-independent IR and state layout are shared by multiple code","Native and WebAssembly execution use the same runtime contract.","The testbench sees the same typed design API regardless of the execution"],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/celox-sim/celox/blob/master/README.md","fingerprint":"c09fa508cb69e8a5fd4703bf83721e8880e865b0bdbe2a254cc63bfcb30ba5b6","extractedAt":"2026-10-03T22:13:41.176Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-04T08:15:58